# Tiled grid evaluation of LC questions · `spec:nrz:lc-grid-tiles`

**Principle (LC evaluation is two graphs joined by bindings)** · `prin:nrz:lc-grid-contract`

An indexed LC evaluation, over a finite grid or an open stream, is a descriptor graph lowered to a computational graph through checked bindings under (`prin:nrz:descriptor-binding-two-graphs`), (`spec:nrz:descriptor-heap-computation`). Exact relation, domain, cover and evidence nodes are shared by their content and dependencies; varying readings and results are heap values bound to indexed symbols. Every tile has a matching heap bitmask. Kernel applications carry tiles and masks through the graph, and decision barriers provide the sole numerical-to-descriptor return path (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-certified-feedback`). Descriptors protect live heap dependencies, including values read across a barrier. Every index's own enclosed answer is resolved through its output binding and mask. Reuse follows (`spec:nrz:reuse-concept`). This document owns the common indexed-domain, certificate, binding, projection, decision and graph-cost rules, then specializes them to finite axes. The open-axis specialization (`spec:nrz:lc-streams`) owns host-filled tile loans, publication and bounded retirement for both shapes, including flush. The requirements below are contracts, including when phrased as present-tense graph behavior. Implementation standing and LC completion are recorded only in (`sec:nrz:lc-closing-register`), particularly (`tab:nrz:lc-closing-tiles-evaluation`); the measured baseline below is historical evidence, not tiled-path acceptance.

## Measured standing · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-standing`

**Observation (Measurement conditions)** · `obs:nrz:lc-grid-measurement-conditions`

A throwaway cost measurement against main revision `ea2a94f`, with measured paths unchanged at source revision `bc1ad28388d2e9ebe268ff01f46ec9e19624b83b`, uses a release build, `f64`, one core of a Ryzen 9 5950X and one run per size. Work is the budget's exact count, distinct from elapsed time. The forward question varies L at every cell, fixes C to `1/4`, requests one time point and has cycle range `[0,0]`; the selected inputs make the L*C endpoint products rational squares. The supplied measurement evidence identifies no measurement date or repeated-run uncertainty, so neither is inferred. The platform scope of this evidence is x86_64 Linux (`dec:vision:supported-platform`).

**Observation (Rebuilding the forward question at every cell)** · `obs:nrz:lc-grid-forward-cost`

For grid populations of 10, 100, 1,000 and 10,000 cells, answering from scratch costs respectively 237, 256, 278 and 270 microseconds per cell, and whole-cell totals of 74,496, 79,071, 83,698 and 88,691 Work per cell. These totals include input construction, relation construction and native projection, as well as the cover. A separate measurement counting only `forward_cover` gives 69,661, 78,782, 83,737 and 88,640 Work per cell at populations 10, 10³, 10⁴ and 10⁵ respectively; its other slices are 2,233 Work for input construction, 5 for relation construction and about 2,600–2,750 for native projection. The two supplied population sequences are retained as measured; these are distinct slices, not paired totals to subtract or a relabelling of the original populations. Growing exact fractions account for the increasing Work; peak memory stays at 4.6 MB. `LcRelation::forward_cover` accounts for about 85 percent of elapsed time, rebuilding the exact cover at each cell; `LcRelation::new` and `LcForwardProjection::new` account for about four percent each. Extrapolating 270 microseconds to a billion cells gives about 75 core-hours; this is an extrapolation, not a measured billion-cell run. The supported forward contract is (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-forward-implementation`).

**Observation (Whole-box and prepared-family controls)** · `obs:nrz:lc-grid-reuse-controls`

Answering one box over the whole L range costs 231 microseconds and 70,369 Work independently of the number of cells represented, but returns one wide enclosure. Reading members from one prepared zero-time cover through `LcTimeProjection::member` costs 7.3 to 8.9 microseconds and 2,213 to 2,800 Work per member. One `LcTimeProjection::tail` call encloses all members from index 15 onward in four microseconds. These controls demonstrate useful family reuse under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-zero-time-implementation`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-overlap-tail`); a wide common enclosure supplies no cell-specific answer, and the measured member route still spends Work after preparation.

**Observation (Rational-square admission defect)** · `obs:nrz:lc-grid-square-refusal`

`LcRelation::forward_cover` returns `LcRelationError::Projection` when either extremal L*C product lacks a rational square root. Its private `inverse_root` in `crates/bindings/src/lc_relation/forward/rectangle.rs` constructs the exact positive root, then requires `as_rational()` before taking its reciprocal. A generic positive L by C grid is therefore refused almost everywhere by this adapter, despite the mathematical frequency being defined. The rational extremal-rate restriction is explicit in (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-forward-implementation`). This domain restriction and the repeated exact work of (`obs:nrz:lc-grid-forward-cost`) are separate defects: speeding up the square-only path does not supply general positive LC evaluation.

## Shared indexed evaluation and finite tiles · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-tile-contract`

**Definition (Indexed domains and heap tiles)** · `def:nrz:lc-grid-indexed-domain`

An indexed evaluation has an exact axis-domain node, indexed input and output symbols, and edges assigning supplied and queried roles in the original LC relation. A finite axis or finite axis product supplies a grid; an open ordered axis supplies a stream under (`def:nrz:lc-stream-axis`). A tile is an nrz-owned heap region with a layout, a matching bitmask and an association to its indexed domain. It is not a physical support certificate. Index-domain inclusion and any proved physical-domain containment are separate relations. Readings, their deterministic bounds and numerical errors arrive as heap values; there is no declared sensor input range and no pre-arrival support gate. A decision over arrived values establishes which mathematical domains, strata and laws apply. Exact domains prepared ahead of time are reusable cached strata, not conditions for accepting a tile. Question schema, roles, axes, meanings, source maps and retained joint constraints still scope every claim. Execution partitions the original relation without replacing it or inferring existence from samples.

**Rule (Tile ownership and mandatory heap masks)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-mask-ownership`

nrz owns tile memory, tile size, layout, bitmasks and their lifetimes. A tile cannot be defined without its matching bitmask, with one bit per cell and the same cell correspondence. Every graph path, including inputs, results, companions, branches and refinement subgraphs, carries that correspondence. Filled cells, branch membership, refused cells and undecided cells are expressed by the relevant mask states: each mask selects a set with one bit per cell, while its graph role supplies the meaning. A single bit does not encode all statuses at once. The memory contract is (`req:nrz:lc-grid-mask-design`), specified by (`def:nrz:tiles-streams-mask-storage`); implementation standing is (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-engine-tiles-masks`). Masks are kernel-owned heap data, never descriptors or a second exact selection collection. Descriptor bindings describe their meanings and preserve referenced region versions without turning individual bits or readings into descriptor nodes. An unfilled cell supplies no value, and no kernel may read it. The host may offer a preferred tile size, for example for latency; nrz chooses the actual size within its own bounds under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-size`).

**Definition (Grid axis and indexed inputs)** · `def:nrz:lc-grid-inputs`

A grid specializes (`def:nrz:lc-grid-indexed-domain`) to an exact finite axis descriptor or a finite Cartesian product of ordered axis descriptors under (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-grid-symbol`). Its domain indexes instances of the original LC relation (`def:nrz:lc-relation`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-any-direction`). Each varying input is an indexed symbol over that domain; its active tile restriction is bound to one native region, with companion symbols bound to their regions. The global axis domain does not require simultaneous materialization of all its numerical selections. Axes may vary L, C, initial phase, query time, phase-time observations, amplitudes, energy or deterministic supports. Supplied and queried roles are edges to the original relation node, not extra inferred constraints. A series adds a sample-axis descriptor and a shared epoch node. Shared uncertainty sources remain shared nodes with their original joint-domain edges under (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-source-symbols`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-two-layer-measurements`); an axis product does not prove independence. Statistical moments alone supply no bounded-support edge (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-certified-sets`).

**Definition (Finite grid tiles and branch tiles)** · `def:nrz:lc-grid-tile`

A finite grid input tile associates its cells with a bounded part of the finite axis ranges under (`def:nrz:lc-grid-indexed-domain`); a sub-box is one possible layout. Its mask records which cells are filled. Branch tiles may group noncontiguous original indices, with bindings preserving their original axis/source association through any new layout. They need not be contiguous index sub-boxes or uniform physical boxes. Checked domain and boundary relations retain every original index without duplicating its outcome, even where physical enclosures overlap. Different source or schema meanings retain their distinct dependencies. Host-filled regions, including arbitrary partial masks, use the same loan and handback edges as stream tiles under (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-input-loan`), (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-handback`), (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-partial-tile`); a grid does not require the engine to synthesize input values.

**Rule (Shared exact authority at barriers)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-tile-certificate`

At a decision barrier, arrived heap results meet the descriptor layer. The decision establishes or reuses evidence connecting their scoped domain to the original relation, complete projection cover, cycle-domain, branch-boundary, physical premises and pi nodes. Each fact has explicit proven, assumed or open standing: physical input assumptions stay assumptions, checked cover/cut facts carry their proofs, and unresolved obligations remain open. A published conclusion has all evidence it requires; an enclosure alone supplies neither existence nor emptiness nor a structural verdict. Parameterized output and companion symbols and their lowering relations share the same scope. A family certificate quantifies over varying input coordinates under (`def:nrz:reuse-lambda`), rather than extending a ground point proof by assertion. Compatible tiles cite that authority through checked domain and dependency relations. Prepared exact domains are only a cache of such strata; their availability neither declares a sensor range nor bypasses post-arrival decisions. For fixed domain, schema, precision and branch complexity, reusable numerical-family authority is independent of index population; discovering actual membership or an index-to-value map has its separately charged barrier cost under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`), (`def:nrz:lc-grid-map-workload`). Uniform branch facts and complete alternative-family nodes retain their distinct meanings under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-feasibility-witness`).

**Rule (Shared nodes and hoisted numerical values)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-shared-nodes`

The original relation, exact pi/full-turn definitions, root-bracket proofs and checked cover/branch schemas are shared nodes across every tile whose domain and dependency edges permit reuse. A child's inclusion proof transports valid parent evidence; tighter cuts add new evidence without copying or rebuilding the parent graph. Exact nodes independent of the grid index lower to a shared numerical prelude under (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-hoisted-constants`), evaluated once per compatible node-and-version key in (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-reuse-key`). Shared symbols may have several checked region bindings; equal float values alone create no sharing relation. A changed guard or source version prevents numerical reuse even if the same symbolic node survives.

**Rule (Authenticated pi authority and numerical prelude)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-pi-reuse`

Cache construction and decision/refinement barriers use eligible graph-authenticated Machin authority under (`[CAS-rule:cas:pi-graph-publication]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:pi-bound-cache-key]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:pi-bound-cache-accounting]`), retaining original-target, context, family and live-dependency checks. A miss may require full validation, and a warm exact citation still consumes Work; both belong to the attempt that performs them. Reusing the same `PiBoundCertificate` object through `LcRelation::forward_cover`, `PiIntervalCover::check` and `pi.check` still runs full finite-sum validation and is not authenticated reuse. The kernel pass between barriers under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`) permits neither full pi validation nor a charged exact pi citation per numerical cell; any needed exact citation is charged at its decision barrier. Hoisting the numerical pi import and prelude evaluation is a separate obligation from sharing exact pi proof authority. Observe full validation, authenticated citations, proof replay, lookup probes/hits/misses/refusals and numerical import/prelude evaluation separately under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-construction-events`). The cold and warm Work in (`[CAS-ex:cas:pi-bound-cache-costs]`) have their own receipt scopes and cannot be subtracted from the distinct measured forward slices.

**Rule (Indexed native bindings with checked overlap)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-array-binding`

All continuously varying values, deterministic bounds and numerical errors are native float arrays bound to indexed tile symbols under (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-grid-symbol`), (`spec:nrz:descriptor-binding`). Masks remain heap data under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-mask-ownership`). Bindings connect symbols and region-version selections many-to-many; every overlap requires a checked reading relation under (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-checked-overlap`), and every heap entry has binding coverage under (`prin:nrz:descriptor-binding-total`). Original source, kind, unit, axis and map dependencies are checked as parameterized families. Handback authenticates ownership, initialization, layout and version correspondence; it does not perform native containment admission against a predeclared sensor box. Whether arrived values satisfy the physical or mathematical premises belongs to their decision barrier. Cycle indices remain exact descriptor-domain parameters outside the floating heap. Between barriers, array entries add no exact rational literals, scalar descriptors or admission edges; native version, mask and layout checks remain visible work. Family bindings must meet (`req:nrz:lc-grid-indexed-binding`); their inspected standing is (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-lc-indexed-bindings`).

**Rule (Output symbols and bindings precede execution)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-output-binding`

Each queried quantity is an output symbol in the descriptor graph indexed over the evaluation axis and restricted by the active tile and mask, with sample or exact cycle axes where its family requires them. Before that output's kernels run, lowering derives its sort, kind, unit, domains and companion-symbol relations and binds it to a reserved indexed result region and matching mask, following (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-result-symbols`), (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-companion-symbols`). Total-bound and numerical-error symbols bind to their companion regions with checked target edges. A decision may first choose the continuation and its layout; its concrete bindings are then established before its kernels execute. Pending bindings become readable only on complete publication of the participating cells and required companions. Results resolve by output symbol, mask and live region version under (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-validation`), without constructing a scalar descriptor per cell. Family membership and branch association are represented by masks with scoped evidence; feasibility is never inferred from numerical centers.

**Rule (Heap pass as a lowered subgraph)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-heap-pass`

Between decision barriers, a tile pass executes the lowered computation subgraph of (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-lowered-subgraph`): kernel-application edges map indexed input-region versions, masks and shared prelude values to output-region, mask and companion versions. Execution walks the graph for runnable leaves under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-ready-leaves`); there is no scheduler. Bounded numerical kernels evaluate participating cells with no exact Work, CAS calls or descriptor construction. Each output selection has its own freshly computed enclosure or family of enclosures from its actual heap inputs, with total deterministic coverage and separately tracked numerical error. Import, arithmetic and restoration error occur once under (`thm:nrz:descriptor-binding-kernel-enclosure`), (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-error-composition`). No remembered value, interpolation or copied wide tile hull supplies the answer. Checked lowering and the preceding barrier's scoped authority justify numerical execution; a new semantic conclusion requires a further decision barrier under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`).

**Rule (Exact work belongs to decision barriers)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`

Kernels between barriers run on tiles and masks with zero exact Work, exact node/edge additions, descriptor constructions and CAS calls. Their numerical laws and semantic premises have checked authority for that continuation; a kernel cannot silently extend it. New post-arrival membership, enclosure/cut replay, stratum construction, exact restriction and selected-attachment publication are decision work charged at the barrier that performs them, not retrospectively called prefill setup. Existing per-arrival lattice origin replay, exact endpoint import, containment and CAS selection still cost Work under (`rule:nrz:decision-enclosure-and-cut`), (`rule:nrz:decision-selected-publication`). Their presence in a current route does not establish this target kernel-only pass. Decisions become fast by content-keyed reuse under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-decision-cache`): a stratum, cut or lemma already decided is a charged lookup rather than a re-proof. A cached numerical cover alone does not select an actual threshold branch; the barrier must authenticate the applicable decision evidence and current dependencies. Native scans, mask production, sorting and version checks remain measured even when evidence is reused. Distinct uncached decisions may still cost in proportion to the population; that cost is exposed at barriers, while repeated content must demonstrate reuse. No zero-work claim includes barrier lookup or proof work.

**Definition (Decision barrier and tiled continuations)** · `def:nrz:lc-grid-barrier`

A decision node is a graph-level barrier defined by its own input dependencies, not by tile boundaries. Several tiles may feed one barrier, or the required inputs may occupy less than a single tile. Its dependants wait until those inputs are ready and the decision publishes their authorization. At that boundary heap results meet the descriptor layer, including the original meanings, ordered quantifiers and enclosure/cut evidence. The node can range from one cached comparison producing one mask to a multi-step subgraph producing many results; this is each node's design. Every dependent subgraph is tiled too. A result may be only a new mask whose kernels read a previous tile, or a new tile with its own mask and layout drawing data from previous subgraph heap regions. Descriptor dependencies retain those older tiles for as long as the continuation reads them under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-lifetime`). Refinement is a subgraph chosen by the decision under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-split`), and flush behavior follows (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-flush`).

**Rule (Content-keyed decision reuse)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-decision-cache`

A barrier looks up already decided strata, cuts and lemmas by their complete mathematical content and authority dependencies. The key retains original target, predicate and boundary convention, ordered quantifiers, context, joint source/map meaning, enclosure premises, representation and proof revision, with current heap/version attachment checked separately wherever required by (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-reuse-key`). Equal float values or a matching schema alone cannot authenticate another source or a stale attachment. A hit cites the checked result and charges lookup, authentication and any new attachment; it does not re-prove the same content. A miss charges construction and checking before publishing reusable authority. Changing premises invalidates only the affected reuse; portable replay retains its independent checks. The cache amortizes post-arrival decisions across repeated content in and across tiles, while numerical answers still use current heap inputs. Cache entries retain reusable authority without retaining obsolete heap occupations, so a completed flush can empty the heap and leave caches warm under (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-flush`).

**Rule (Walk the graph for runnable leaves)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-ready-leaves`

Execution walks the graph for leaves that can run, meaning nodes whose inputs are available. Kernel inputs include the matching masks and live bound regions. A barrier holds its dependants back until its own required inputs and decision result are ready; it imposes no unrelated tile-wide or stream-wide holdback. Independent ready leaves may proceed within nrz's resource bounds. There is no scheduler, scheduling policy or refinement engine layered over this walk. Readiness, dependency release and flush notifications must meet (`req:nrz:lc-grid-walk-design`); their implementation standing is (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-engine-walk-flush`).

**Rule (Node identity and version-dependent authority)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-lifetime`

Descriptors protect referenced heap values from collection, including across decision nodes. A mask-only continuation or a new tile drawing from earlier regions retains those regions, their masks and needed companions through its descriptor dependencies until the last reader releases them. Reuse follows node identity, complete domain/context/source/map meaning and numerical versions under (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-reuse-key`). Replacing an input or companion version invalidates dependent live output bindings and decision attachments through (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-invalidation`), (`rule:nrz:decision-invalidation`), even when center bytes agree. An immutable relation, lemma or cut survives when its premises still apply; it does not authenticate a new numerical version. Appending disjoint host input leaves unrelated versions intact. Compatible information refinement and changed assumptions retain distinct edges under (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-refinement`), (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-invalidation`). Output, mask and companion bindings publish atomically under (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-atomic-publication`); selected strata publish with checked evidence under (`rule:nrz:decision-selected-publication`). Failed publication leaves affected outputs unavailable, and historical theorem nodes retain their scoped authority under (`rule:nrz:decision-attachment-lifetime`). Retirement and flush release obsolete heap dependencies without discarding reusable cached proofs.

**Rule (Refinement is a decision subgraph)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-split`

A decision node may choose refinement when its arrived inputs do not settle a cycle, branch, threshold or enclosure obligation. Refinement is itself a tiled subgraph with masks, kernel edges and further decision barriers, not an engine policy. It may tighten evidence, change numerical precision within its allowance, partition physical domains or produce branch tiles grouping original indices. Checked inclusion, parent-cover and boundary-ownership evidence is required where child mathematical domains are introduced. Children reuse original relation, valid source, pi, bracket and ancestor authority through the cache; new proof work is charged at their barriers. Enclosure tightening for the same exact target and joint family follows (`rule:nrz:decision-refinement`), (`spec:nrz:lattice-decisions`), (`rule:nrz:lattice-boundary-selection`); those rules alone do not prove a new subdivision cover. Settled masks retain their evidence while unresolved cells enter the chosen subgraph. Conservative enclosures can cause proof-induced refinement even where the true answer is constant, so its graph growth is measured separately. Arbitrary host arrays sort into branch tiles under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-cell-decisions`); they need not become singleton descriptor domains merely because their source order alternates.

**Rule (Termination and undecided strata)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-stop`

Each decision's refinement subgraph has finite allowances for cumulative exact Work, numerical work, bytes, depth and attempts, together with allowed formats and deterministic error goals. The node chooses whether to spend them on refinement or publish an undecided mask with its remaining obligations. A singleton's physical uncertainty can still straddle a boundary; physical child domains retain inclusion and cover edges to that same joint support, not new independent sources. Increasing precision cannot remove physical uncertainty. Refinement ends at complete evidence or an applicable limit. Undecided cells retain their last valid enclosure and a typed reason such as `BoundaryIntersection`, `UnsupportedCertificate`, `RefinementExhausted` or `DecisionBudgetExhausted` under (`def:nrz:decision-outcomes`); without a finite justified enclosure, the complete candidate domain remains explicit. Their mask asserts no complement membership or guessed branch (`rule:nrz:decision-undecided-continuation`). Invalid meanings, stale dependencies, false evidence and representation failures have distinct refusal masks and unavailable output bindings. These states do not create a separate selection structure or a global refinement policy.

**Definition (Cost as graph growth and numerical evaluation)** · `def:nrz:lc-grid-cost`

Let N be the evaluated cell population, T the execution-tile count, D the distinct reusable authority domains, R the refinement attempts, S the decision-barrier attempts and B the maximum resident cell population. For a stream, N is a finite observed domain, never an infinite materialization. Exact Work charges construction, checking, comparison and reuse at the barriers that perform them, including failed attempts and cache lookups that add no retained node. Optional precomputed strata have separately reported cache-construction cost. Bit size, branch-domain complexity, proof precision and map dimensions under (`def:nrz:lc-grid-map-workload`) remain explicit. Distinguish cold construction, warm lookup/authentication, attachment, refinement and publication rather than treating all tile work as constant setup. Between barriers, (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`) requires zero exact nodes/edges, exact Work, CAS calls and descriptor constructions per cell. The computational graph grows by tile-region versions, masks and bounded kernel-application edges, not a cloned exact question for each cell. Arithmetic, scanning, mask operations, sorting and native version checks remain visible time. At fixed sample/output/branch extent, full numerical output requires work proportional to evaluated cells; heap storage is proportional to B plus bounded scratch and shared prelude regions. Total cost also includes actual map, barrier and refinement sums. Arbitrary inputs may have many distinct uncached decisions, but repeated content shares cached strata and branch tiles instead of repeated proofs. Population-sized masks are heap storage, not population-sized descriptor selections. Stream latency specializes this accounting under (`def:nrz:lc-stream-cost`), (`def:nrz:lc-stream-latency`); cumulative construction, resident graph size and exported evidence are separate measurements.

**Rule (Bounded live subgraphs and retained evidence)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-streaming`

Finite-grid evaluation uses the bounded live graph of (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-bounded-resources`), with the same host loans, concurrent tiles, atomic bindings, flush and evidence retirement under (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-inflight`), (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-flush`), (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-retirement`). The finite parent axis preserves original index identity; graph dependencies determine readiness and consumers can resolve output order through those identities. Full materialized outputs and exported evidence have explicit storage costs outside bounded resident execution. Region decisions in the certified-map class of (`def:nrz:lc-grid-map-workload`) can evaluate covering-box bindings directly when their map and cover evidence justify it. Arbitrary host-filled arrays sort into branch tiles with measured scans and barrier work. This rule delegates the lifetime and publication mechanism to the stream concept; (`req:nrz:lc-grid-bounded-execution`) is its finite-axis application.

**Rule (Tile size changes cost within one question graph)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-size`

nrz decides tile size and layout from its own bounds and the host's optional preference hint. The hint can express a latency preference; it cannot impose a fixed-size override. Heap and scratch capacity, output-family extent, error growth and the applicable graph evidence constrain the choice. Smaller tiles can reduce fill latency and overestimation; larger tiles can amortize kernel and barrier costs. A tile boundary does not define a decision barrier, and resizing does not alter its input dependencies. Node-chosen refinement may change tiling under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-split`). Size never changes the original question, deterministic uncertainty, branch coverage, boundary convention or requested tolerance. Enclosures may differ within the same tracked-error contract; exhausted allowances may leave an undecided mask, never incompatible certified facts (`thm:nrz:decision-reproducibility`). Host-filled grids and streams share the fill/amortization tradeoff of (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-tile-size`).

## Exact frequency without rational roots · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-frequency`

**Rule (Positive-root expression and its numerical binding)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-exact-frequency`

For L>0 and C>0 the descriptor graph retains the exact operation `omega0=(1 rad)/sqrt(L*C)`, with operand edges to the indexed L/C symbols and positivity, root and reciprocal-domain evidence under (`def:nrz:lc-relation`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-kinds`). Its coherent-rate node is `a=omega0/(1 rad)`. Rational inputs can give an exact algebraic root; varying inputs use the same expression over its scoped parameter domain. Rational normalization is optional. Before execution, the frequency and error-companion symbols bind to indexed result regions; the heap evaluates product, positive root and reciprocal with outward error propagation. A numerical root never replaces the exact operation node, and a rational bracket is an enclosure-evidence node for that expression, not a surrogate expression. Rational squares remain simplifications, never admission preconditions.

**Rule (Certified rational brackets settle cover cuts)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-root-brackets`

For an independent positive box with rational coherent product bounds `0<p_min<=L*C<=p_max`, exact arithmetic can check positive rationals r and s satisfying `r*r<=p_min` and `p_max<=s*s`. Thus `sqrt(L*C)` lies in `[r,s]` and a in `[1/s,1/r]`; tighter endpoint brackets can be certified by the same squared inequalities. For correlated inputs these product bounds are outer coverage under the retained joint premises. Combine such rational bounds with independently checked pi bounds to enclose the original lifted phase divided by `2*pi rad`. Exact rational comparisons then certify endpoint cycle indices and completeness of the intervening range using the original floor convention (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-cycle-branches`), (`rule:nrz:lattice-affine-enclosure`), (`rule:nrz:lattice-floor-selection`). Signed time and correlated phase inputs need their own whole-box extrema under the same original constraints. Bracket overlap with a cut triggers refinement, a split or undecided; equality at an irrational face needs an exact identity or other checked equality proof, not a tolerance. Bracketing permits conservative complete covers without falsely claiming that every enclosed endpoint or branch is attained.

**Rule (Frequency proof and execution edges retain their owners)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-frequency-owners`

CAS checks positive-root, squared-bracket, pi, complete cycle-domain and cut evidence at decision barriers; these are exact nodes and proof edges shared by compatible domains. Bindings checks original physical correspondence, quantity kinds, source maps and the relation between indexed symbols and lowered numerical laws. Scalar supplies product, root, reciprocal and pi enclosure laws; matrix validates native input/companion versions and coverage and executes their kernel edges. Feasibility, emptiness, overlap-tail and horizon facts need their own evidence edges from the original domain, not merely a nonempty bracket (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-feasibility-witness`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-overlap-tail`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-forward-horizon`). Generic irrational-root cover edges and their parameterized numerical lowering must meet (`req:nrz:lc-grid-irrational-cover`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-family-kernels`); standing is recorded in (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-nonsquare-frequency`).

## LC questions and decisions · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-questions`

**Rule (Time to phase series binding)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-forward-series`

For supplied L, C, initial phase and a time-series symbol, the decision barrier establishes or reuses scoped evidence for the positive or quiescent domain, complete lifted-phase/cycle cover, timing correlations and applicable forward-horizon derivation. The output is a phase symbol indexed over the product of the tile axis and a second, sample axis, with branch-family structure where wrapping requires multiple arcs. Lowering binds that symbol and its deterministic-bound and numerical-error companions to indexed result regions before kernels run. The lowered subgraph shares its frequency evaluation across the sample axis where dependencies permit, then computes each selected phase enclosure from its own inputs with exact wrap-boundary ownership. A requested horizon is a separate output symbol with finite, infinite or open-domain evidence. The sample-axis and shared-clock nodes remain attached under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-time-queries`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-forward-horizon`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-two-layer-measurements`); the independent fixture is (`ex:nrz:lc-relation-forward-input-boxes`). Changes of cycle or whole-circle regime are decided at barriers, which may choose refinement through (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-split`).

**Rule (Inductance output and alias-domain binding)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-inductance-query`

For a query in L, a decision barrier establishes or reuses evidence for projection of the original observation and supplied-constraint graph, complete rate/product alias domains, positive divisors and remaining scale directions. The output L symbol is indexed over the tile axis and, when necessary, the retained alias-family domain, with total-bound and numerical-error symbols bound before execution to corresponding result regions. Shared product P and rate nodes retain their relation to the original observations. Supplied positive C permits `L=P/C`; positive amplitude ratio R permits `L=R*sqrt(P)`; energy and positive current amplitude permit `L=2*E/Ihat^2`. Checked lowering connects those operations to a heap subgraph using each input selection, preserving every other supplied constraint and error companion. The precise meanings and fixtures are (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-any-direction`), (`ex:nrz:lc-relation-known-element`), (`ex:nrz:lc-relation-amplitude-energy`). Energy alone leaves a free-scale node and its evidence under (`arg:nrz:lc-relation-energy-identifiability`), rather than an invented bounded L binding.

**Rule (Capacitance output and common-product binding)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-capacitance-query`

For a query in C, a decision barrier establishes or reuses evidence for the original joint observation/alias graph and elimination domains. The output C symbol is indexed over the tile and retained alias axes, bound at lowering with its total-bound and numerical-error companions. Supplied positive L permits `C=P/L`; positive amplitude ratio R permits `C=sqrt(P)/R`; energy and positive voltage amplitude permit `C=2*E/Vhat^2`. Their kernel edges compute the indexed C enclosure regions from actual input versions. Simultaneous L and C outputs share the same product/rate and source nodes; two marginal bindings introduce no independence edge (`ex:nrz:lc-relation-known-element`), (`ex:nrz:lc-relation-amplitude-energy`). The restricted capacitor-interval route of (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-capacitance-implementation`) is evidence for its own graph, not a generic family lowering or a universal determinacy theorem.

**Rule (Phase to indexed time-family binding)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-inverse-time`

For a target-phase symbol and epoch node, a decision barrier establishes or reuses evidence for the original joint phase/rate relation, complete cycle-index domain, ordering or ambiguous-numbering facts and any justified overlap tail. The output is an indexed time-family node over the tile axis and exact cycle domain. A member is a projection node of that family, and a tail is a subfamily node with its covering or existence evidence and a start-time output symbol. Their schemas and requested numerical selections bind before execution to result and error-companion regions; the heap pass does not construct one exact member node per cell. On an admitted branch, kernel edges enclose `t_n=(theta_target-theta0+2*pi*n rad)/omega0`. The independent rectangle specializes to `I_n=[2*pi*n/a_max,2*pi*n/a_min]` only for `n>=0`, `a_min>0` and exact starting and target phase zero under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-time-queries`); correlated inferred phase/rate retains its shared constraint nodes. The existing nonnegative member adapter is not extended to signed inverse-time evaluation by this specialization. Tail status and exact-frequency exceptions follow (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-overlap-tail`), (`conv:nrz:lc-relation-tail-status`). Finite numerical selections cannot truncate an unbounded family domain. `LcTimeProjection` supplies existing member/tail semantics; indexed numerical lowering without residual per-member exact work must meet (`req:nrz:lc-grid-family-kernels`), with standing recorded in (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-tiled-grid`).

**Rule (Projection verdicts are scoped evidence facts)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-projection-verdicts`

Structural determinacy and numerical certainty are separate facts attached to projection-domain or stratum nodes under (`def:nrz:lc-relation-determinacy`), (`def:nrz:lc-relation-certainty`). A cached cover has no per-index feasibility consequence without a parameterized witness or existence edge scoped to that input family. Alias-family nodes retain their original-constraint and verdict dependencies. Positive-amplitude and quiescent domain nodes obey (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-kinds`): at rest the frequency binding can remain defined while physical oscillation phase has a domain-outcome node. Certified empty, underdetermined, defined-domain, undecided and refused strata have distinct evidence and availability consequences. Tightened supports and added observations preserve information by inclusion and proof edges under (`req:nrz:lc-relation-refinement-monotonicity`), including open obligations after exhausted refinement.

**Definition (Decision predicate and boundary nodes)** · `def:nrz:lc-grid-predicate`

A decision node declares its exact predicate, threshold symbol, units, ordered quantifier structure, index/window domain and retained joint physical-family domain before evaluation. The quantifier structure identifies each bound index or physical variable, its scope and dependencies, including conditional fibres; it distinguishes, for example, `forall i exists s P(i,s)` from `exists s forall i P(i,s)` for one shared source s. Unsupported structures are refused, never reordered or replaced by independent marginal domains. Its partition node connects to shared threshold, cycle-face and branch-cut nodes with strict/inclusive ownership under (`def:nrz:decision-partition`), (`rule:nrz:decision-cut-predicates`). It consumes authenticated output-enclosure bindings and the exact partition; a kernel comparison alone cannot add a semantic fact. At a declared universal quantifier, yes requires complete coverage and no a feasible counterexample; at an existential quantifier, yes requires a feasible witness and no complete exclusion, with inner quantified obligations discharged in their declared scope. A singleton is a special domain, not a different mechanism. Empty physical domains have explicit outcome nodes rather than vacuous physical assertions. Every conclusion retains separate enclosure and cut or witness evidence under (`rule:nrz:decision-enclosure-and-cut`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-feasibility-witness`).

**Rule (Aggregation preserves shared-source quantifier order)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-quantifier-order`

For the same retained joint family, per-index universal physical coverage can establish `forall i forall s P(i,s)`, and a feasible counterexample refutes that assertion. Per-index feasible witnesses establish `forall i exists s P(i,s)` only with their index-dependent witness scopes; they do not establish a common realization. A claim `exists s forall i P(i,s)` requires one joint witness s satisfying every indexed obligation and all original source constraints, or a checked equivalent joint existence proof. With shared `s in [-1,1]`, the predicates `P(0,s): s>0` and `P(1,s): s<0` each have a witness, yet no common s satisfies both. Decision covers, continuations and aggregate conclusions retain this ordered structure; any valid quantifier exchange needs its own proof. Stream persistence under source narrowing additionally follows (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-decision-scope`).

**Rule (Decision masks and branch tiles)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-cell-decisions`

A decision barrier partitions the arrived cells of its declared inputs into branch and outcome masks with checked coverage and disjointness. Certified-yes and certified-no masks carry the applicable descriptor-layer enclosure/cut or witness evidence; undecided masks retain open obligations, last-enclosure bindings and typed reasons under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-stop`). Refusal masks preserve typed failures and output unavailability. Mask bits remain heap data; the semantic strata and their reusable proofs are cached by content under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-decision-cache`). A uniform conclusion is shared only when proved for its complete quantified scope. Nonuniform host arrays sort into branch tiles, preserving original indices and source associations, rather than being refused or degraded because their physical hull straddles a cut. The node chooses a new mask over previous data or a new tile/layout under (`def:nrz:lc-grid-barrier`). Kernels scan or move values as needed, and post-arrival decisions, cache misses and attachments remain charged at the barrier. Later tiled continuations depend on the selected evidence under (`rule:nrz:decision-stratification`), (`rule:nrz:decision-stratum-sharing`); they cannot justify the enclosure that selected them. Undecided is not a mathematical third complement (`rule:nrz:decision-undecided-continuation`). The traceability obligations of (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-outcome-view`) survive sorting and mask-only continuation.

**Rule (Original outcomes resolve through bindings and masks)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-outcome-view`

For indexed families, this contract refines (`def:nrz:decision-outcome-table`) and (`rule:nrz:decision-batch-strata`): stable original order and point identity, parent stratum, decision identity, exact case/parameter association, actual input and companion versions, evidence, attempt history, selected option or undecided/refusal reason, downstream activation and output availability all remain resolvable. The original key and ordinal are a view of the axis-to-original association, not a second exact cell-result collection. Given an original index and decision node, checked axis/source/layout maps resolve the cell in its current tile, its heap masks resolve the applicable branch or outcome, and descriptor dependencies resolve the authority and actual bound versions. Shared attempt/evidence nodes retain failed dependencies and inactive alternatives. New semantic attachments are charged at barriers; reading an already published outcome performs no new proof. Any materialized outcome table is an optional charged view of these graph paths, including enumeration, bytes and version validation, never a second source of truth. Existing routes lacking this family contract retain their requirements; shared templates alone do not establish it.

**Rule (Region decision graph and covering evidence)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-region-decisions`

A region decision is a barrier over a declared index/window or continuous physical domain, with ordered quantifiers and the retained joint family under (`def:nrz:lc-grid-predicate`), (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-quantifier-order`). Conclusions retain the scope of (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-decision-scope`), including after flush. Bound covering enclosures feed a checked partition with branch/verdict evidence and outcome masks. At the relevant quantified scope, complete yes coverage proves universal yes and a feasible counterexample proves its no; a feasible witness proves existential yes and complete exclusion proves its no. Inner obligations and shared-source witness scopes cannot be exchanged while aggregating. Unresolved masks retain open obligations without forcing an aggregate verdict. Settled interiors avoid index enumeration only where the certified map and cover evidence of (`def:nrz:lc-grid-map-workload`) justify it. Arbitrary arrays instead scan and sort into branch tiles; noncontiguous original membership is valid and fully traceable. A finite grid partition has no continuous-region cover edge merely because all samples decide (`rule:nrz:decision-membership-locality`), (`rule:nrz:decision-batch-strata`). Requested point outputs have matching masks and result/companion bindings with explicit materialization cost. The enclosure/cut standard remains unchanged; new child-domain covers are established by the decision's refinement subgraph under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-split`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-region-evidence`).

**Definition (Certified-map class for boundary-sensitive cost)** · `def:nrz:lc-grid-map-workload`

A claim that region cost follows boundary geometry declares an authenticated index-to-value map `F(i,s)` for complete input supports and joint source variables s, checked regional image enclosures and a cover-complexity bound for the chosen geometry and resolution. One possible class is a fixed-dimensional, finitely described piecewise map whose regional image and threshold evidence proves a cover bound `K0+K_boundary(N,epsilon)`: K0 bounds settled pieces independently of interior population, and K_boundary bounds unresolved pieces at resolution epsilon. This is an evidence-backed workload premise, not a consequence of ordered axes, monotonicity alone or a common physical hull. Report map description size, pieces, source/dependency complexity, index/coefficient bit sizes, discovery/validation Work and time, image/membership cost, cover depth and retained/exported bytes. Include any scan establishing that host data follows the map. Uniform physical support, a uniform decision outcome and compact index geometry are distinct properties. Arbitrary host arrays need no such map for ordinary tiled evaluation: they use charged scans and barrier decisions, cached strata and heap masks to sort noncontiguous members into branch tiles. The special map claim only justifies skipping enumeration; it is not an admission condition or a quality tier. Distinct uncached semantic content can require population-sized proof work, while repeated branch content reuses authority under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-decision-cache`).

**Counterexample (Alternating settled interiors)** · `cex:nrz:lc-grid-alternating-interiors`

Take a host-filled one-dimensional grid with `C=1`, `t=1`, `theta0=0`, positive energy and L alternating between `1` and `1/9`. The physical phase alternates between one and three radians, so every singleton support is away from a threshold of two radians, but every interval containing both values has a straddling physical hull. Restricting execution to contiguous sub-box refinement would need singleton index tiles despite no physical support touching that threshold. The decision barrier instead forms the two branch masks and can sort them into branch tiles, reusing the two comparisons by content and preserving original indices. The scan, mask production, sorting and charged lookups remain visible; neither refusal nor a weaker answer follows from alternating input order. A certified parity map could justify compact noncontiguous membership and avoid some enumeration, but discovering or validating it against arbitrary host entries is charged map work under (`def:nrz:lc-grid-map-workload`). This still forbids an unconditional claim that total work depends only on physical boundary cells.

## Acceptance measurement · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-acceptance`

**Requirement (Rerun the measured workload over tile graphs)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-benchmark`

Rerun (`obs:nrz:lc-grid-measurement-conditions`) through arrived-value decision barriers and the lowered tiled heap subgraphs at populations 10, 100, 1,000 and successive powers of ten through 10⁹ cells under (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-scale-measurement`). Record actual executed populations and stops separately from projections; a smaller run leaves billion-cell acceptance unmet. Supply the theoretical reference, predicted budget, component tracking and measured bound of (`sec:nrz:lc-closing-certification-cost`). Cover every question kind in (`sec:nrz:lc-grid-questions`), both decision forms, rational-square controls and generic positive non-square L*C inputs. Use one pinned release `f64`, single-core timing environment for comparability and both supported numerical formats for correctness. Identify actual hardware, toolchain, arithmetic revisions and policies. Use repeated independent timings with raw observations, warm/cold setup state and a declared interval-estimation method; the one-run baseline supplies no retrospective intervals. Include domains sharing cached authority, arbitrary host arrays sorted into branch tiles, tile-capacity crossings, cycle/branch/threshold boundaries, exact equality, correlated supports, quiescent domains, aliases and exhausted-refinement strata. Hold sample-axis extent and numerical branch/output extent fixed for primary coarse scaling comparisons, with separate controls varying those dimensions. All measurement outputs belong to acceptance evidence; they are not the representation of LC results. The timing and correctness environments stay within the support scope of (`dec:vision:supported-platform`).

**Requirement (Coarse scaling analysis and cost attribution)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-fit`

Benchmark each question kind at fixed workload and numerical/output complexity, reporting raw independent timings, direct setup cost, kernel time, barrier time, cache state and memory. Coarse plots and, where useful, a fit such as `t(N)=k+c*N^p` describe observed scaling; report units, setup treatment, residuals and uncertainty honestly, with c a per-cell cost only when p is one. Report authority-domain and tile counts, extents, capacities, exact constructions, lookup hits/misses, refinement attempts and actual setup/decision sums. Nonconstant tile, map, barrier and refinement costs cannot be hidden in a constant intercept: expose those sums and distinct capacity regimes, for example with `t_total(N)=k+sum_j setup_j(N)+c*N^p`. Use coarse analysis to identify behavior that looks out of place and investigate those cases further. There is no linear-scaling equivalence band, exponent pass threshold or mandatory fine statistical acceptance test. Independent runs support any reported intervals; correlated cells are not independent timing repetitions, and censored waits/stops remain visible. Accounting and semantic evidence, rather than a fitted exponent, determine acceptance.

**Requirement (Graph counters, memory and exhaustive domain coverage)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-counters`

Count exact nodes and edges, CAS invocations, descriptor constructions and Work at cache preparation and each decision/refinement barrier, including failures and charged hits. Zero exact work between barriers requires (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`) and the observation contracts of (`req:nrz:lc-grid-accounting-coverage`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-phase-receipts`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-construction-events`). Count native region versions, masks and kernel-application edges separately, together with arithmetic, scans, sorting, layout and version checks. Report heap bytes per resident cell, scratch, resident descriptor nodes/bytes, live authority references, absolute peak and exported output/evidence storage as N increases at fixed capacity. Checked masks and their scoped cover evidence account once for every participating original index across completed, undecided and refused outcomes; unfilled cells remain distinguishable. Report reasons including unsupported evidence, boundary, exhausted allowance, numerical range, invalid meaning and stale dependencies; non-square input and boundary geometry are independent workload properties, not automatic refusals. Failed and open decisions stay in timing denominators. Include descriptor-protected older tiles, masks, companions, ready-walk storage, cache size and historical registry/schema/provenance owners. Report the empty heap and retained warm caches after full flush separately.

**Requirement (Accounting coverage for every measured route)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-accounting-coverage`

The acceptance evidence includes a coverage manifest for every entry point and helper traversed by each measured route, identifying caller-visible, private or discarded budget ledgers, charged operations, unmetered operations and observer coverage. A zero returned Work delta or no retained graph growth alone proves absence of neither symbolic reconstruction nor authentication nor copying. Explicitly cover context construction in `crates/bindings/src/engine.rs`, fresh private checking budgets in `crates/bindings/src/checker.rs`, `Binding::validate` and its immutable-budget allowance checks, symbol import preparation in `crates/matrix/src/binding/symbols.rs`, scalar `Enclosure` arithmetic/reductions, heap import/replacement/retirement, layout/shape validation and descriptor construction where used. Lattice freezing, enclosure, selection, preparation and publication take caller budgets and charge work even when they expose no `usage()` result; absence of that accessor does not make them unmetered. Observe deep owned clones, destruction and allocation events, capacity and bytes separately from conservative Work tariffs. The manifest is evidence about these routes, not the LC result model. A repository-wide public manifest, general public accounting API and expanded allocator inventory remain the accounting owners' separate scope; their absence cannot excuse gaps on a claimed route.

**Requirement (Exclusive phase receipts and honest remainder)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-phase-receipts`

Provide exclusive receipts, or equivalent auditable snapshots on each same ledger, for preparation, proof generation/replay, lookup/authentication, native validation/evaluation and publication, with explicit remainder and refusal accounting. Phase totals plus remainder equal the observed ledger delta without double counting nested calls; retain private/discarded ledger deltas separately and explain cross-ledger attribution. Preserve spent Work on refused attempts and leave all existing Work pins unchanged. Separate unmetered time/events from charged Work rather than inventing a tariff. Place snapshots at the actual cache-construction, handback, barrier lookup/proof, refinement-subgraph, publication, flush and replay boundaries of (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`). The attribution fixture and production observer must share the boundary-span and same-ledger subtraction contract (`req:nrz:tiles-streams-work-trace-layer`); their inspected implementation and LC route coverage are recorded in (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-cost-accounting`). Any observer used for bounded-residence claims has its own measured storage/time and cannot hide an unbounded trace.

**Requirement (Construction and reuse events)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-construction-events`

Observe attempted, completed and retained descriptor, exact-node, evidence-edge and certificate constructions, including transient or rolled-back work. Separate full pi validations, graph-authenticated exact citations, portable and local proof replays, lookup probes/hits/misses/refusals and numerical pi import/prelude evaluations under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-pi-reuse`). Count meaningful deep copies, allocations, capacity growth, retirement and destruction with bytes/time where applicable; also expose mask scans, sorting, native layout checks and source/map/version authentication, with mathematical containment charged at its decision barrier. Net graph growth and cache-hit counts alone are insufficient. Zero prohibited events between barriers must agree with the phase receipts and coverage manifest, while permitted native work remains reported.

**Requirement (Setup, answer and replay claim scopes)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-claim-scopes`

Every setup, answer or replay receipt identifies its original relation/family, index and physical coverage, source/map/companion versions, authority revision and returned claim. A whole-box cover, a fresh point-specific numerical answer, a selected decision and a portable proof replay have separate scopes even when they cite one certificate. The acceptance scenario caches one exact domain stratum, separately obtains its whole-box cover and fresh point-specific answers from arrived inputs, then demonstrates zero prohibited construction/replay events between barriers under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`), while reporting barrier lookups, new decisions and native checks. Exercise values beyond the cached domain without treating that domain as a sensor range: the barrier decides them through other cached strata or charged new evidence. Mutate an original source, map and unchanged-center companion and exercise an equal-valued foreign source; stale or false authority must refuse or be correctly revalidated. Unchanged numerical values cannot authenticate changed dependencies. Report portable proof replay independently. Use the zero-time family as a positive control for cover reuse with its nonzero exact member Work visible, the spectral batch as a control for shared schema/prelude and live validation only, repeated current `forward_cover` as a negative control for pi reuse, and graph-authenticated Machin authority as its positive control. None of these controls alone proves general indexed decisions.

**Requirement (Joined decision-attempt evidence)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-decision-attempts`

Decision workloads join observations of cache preparation, barrier lookup/authentication, any origin replay and containment proof, selection, refinement and selected publication for the same original question, dependency versions and attempt. Retain failed and undecided attempts with their stop/refusal reasons, spent resources and last valid enclosure; a successful selected attachment does not erase earlier costs. Existing lattice snapshots can supply this evidence where complete under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-accounting-coverage`). A general public lattice report belongs to its owner and is not a prerequisite to forward-only controls, but full decision acceptance requires this joined observation and the enclosure/cut and atomic-publication obligations of (`rule:nrz:decision-enclosure-and-cut`), (`rule:nrz:decision-selected-publication`).

**Requirement (Small reproducible accounting fixtures)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-accounting-fixtures`

Publish small reference fixtures at at least two populations with exact input generation, the full L sequence or a unique generator, pi certificate revision/content, source and arithmetic revisions, snapshot positions, claim scopes and per-cell Work vectors. The measured conditions of (`obs:nrz:lc-grid-measurement-conditions`) do not uniquely determine the throwaway baseline's exact inputs or certificate; preserve its figures without claiming exact Work reproduction until those data are supplied, and identify any replacement comparison fixture as such. Raw timings supplement these fixtures and do not replace them. This requirement is scoped acceptance evidence, not a demand for a general benchmarking framework.

**Requirement (Decision strata and boxes against boundary share)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-boundary-measurement`

For the certified-map class of (`def:nrz:lc-grid-map-workload`), justify regional image and cover-complexity assumptions, then measure map preparation/validation/query costs, covering-box evaluations, settled/unresolved masks, child depth and exact nodes/edges/Work against N and cells near the true decision boundary. Define that neighborhood from physical support, resolution and certified error, not observed failures. Vary resolution, interior volume and boundary complexity independently, including wholly settled outcomes and exact faces. Any claim following a cover bound includes every map cost and distinguishes region evaluation from optional output materialization. For arbitrary host arrays and streams, report input scans, mask production, branch sorting, barrier cache hits/misses and distinct proof content without rejecting or degrading the workload for its input order. Include (`cex:nrz:lc-grid-alternating-interiors`) separately from the case where all physical supports straddle the boundary. Physical-support uniformity, decision-outcome uniformity and index-cover geometry remain separately reported; none is a proxy for another.

**Criterion (Semantic structure and observable cost determine acceptance)** · `crit:nrz:lc-grid-structural-acceptance`

Acceptance requires complete cost attribution through (`req:nrz:lc-grid-accounting-coverage`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-phase-receipts`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-construction-events`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-claim-scopes`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-decision-attempts`) and (`req:nrz:lc-grid-accounting-fixtures`). Those observations substantiate zero exact Work, exact constructions and CAS calls between decision barriers, charged post-arrival decisions and cache reuse instead of re-proving identical content. Require bounded resident heap/descriptors at fixed capacity, matching masks throughout the graph, descriptor retention of older live tiles, complete outcome coverage and empty heap with warm caches after full flush. Uniform reusable-semantic controls expose repeated exact authority construction; arbitrary arrays must sort into branch tiles with honest scan, sorting and barrier costs. Region enumeration may be omitted only under the proved map/cover premises of (`req:nrz:lc-grid-boundary-measurement`). Generic positive non-square inputs cannot be refused merely for irrationality. Independent oracles, mutation and boundary controls verify semantics alongside accounting. Scaling is benchmarked and coarsely analyzed under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-fit`), without a fitted-exponent target or scaling-equivalence band. The explained relative-cost bound is also required under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-ratio-information`), (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-overhead-tracking`), (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-overhead-bound`); meeting it does not replace these semantic and accounting obligations. These acceptance requirements do not assert measured delivery; (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-tiled-grid`), (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-scale-measurement`) record their standing.

**Rule (Certification overhead uses a theoretical compute reference)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-ratio-information`

Use the theoretical optimized binary64 compute requirement of (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-naive-model`) as the sole overhead reference for each measured question; no naive implementation is built or measured for that reference. Count operations, traffic and vector width, source the machine throughput and memory assumptions, and derive the binding roofline cost. Against that model, predict the labelled about-four-to-eightfold budget (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-overhead-budget`), measure each component through counters and sampled times with intervals (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-overhead-tracking`), and meet the about-tenfold-or-less bound on actual runs through 10⁹ cells (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-overhead-bound`). Historical measured plain-function ratios remain observations, not this reference. Distinguish setup, barrier lookup/proof, refinement, output materialization and exact work amortized over actual tile cells; report a small, stated unexplained remainder. A ratio above the bound or an unexplained departure from a component prediction leaves the cost claim unmet. The comparison cannot weaken semantic coverage or exclude unsuccessful attempts, and adds no absolute latency, memory-envelope or target-environment gate. The support claim remains limited to x86_64 Linux (`dec:vision:supported-platform`).

## Reusable structures and implementation obligations · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-engine`

**Rule (Existing covers become shared graph authority)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-existing-covers`

`LcRelation`, `LcForwardCover`, `LcForwardProjection` and pair/scale projection owners provide existing relation, cover and branch authority under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-forward-implementation`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-pairs-implementation`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-scale-implementation`). Decision barriers reference that authority under its original premises rather than duplicating it per index. `LcTimeProjection` supplies complete member/tail semantics and existence strength under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-zero-time-implementation`). Those scoped owners do not establish the combined indexed live-binding, family-evidence, mask, barrier-cache and zero-exact-work contract; (`obs:nrz:lc-grid-reuse-controls`) records the measured member Work. Family nodes must share member/tail projection schemas without exact construction at every numerical selection. (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-tiled-grid`) records standing, including subsequently admitted engine prerequisites.

